@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+---
+id: task-001-implement-json-pointer-rfc-6901-resolvepointer
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) resolvePointer"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement RFC 6901 JSON Pointer resolution: export `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from
+`src/index.ts`, returning the value a pointer refers to, and throwing when the pointer is
+syntactically invalid or does not resolve to a value in the document. This is the first of a
+series of features that build on each other; later features will depend on this one.
+
+## What I did
+
+- Wrote `src/index.test.ts` first (red), covering: the empty pointer resolving to the whole
+  document, every example pointer from RFC 6901 §5 (including `~0`/`~1` escaping and the quirky
+  keys `""`, `"a/b"`, `"c%d"`, etc.), nested object/array traversal, and the error paths — a
+  pointer not starting with `/`, a missing object member, an out-of-bounds array index, the `-`
+  array index (valid for JSON Patch append, not for resolution), a zero-padded array index
+  (`"00"`), and descending into a scalar.
+- Implemented `resolvePointer` in `src/index.ts`: splits the pointer on `/`, decodes each
+  reference token (`~1` → `/` then `~0` → `~`, in that order per the RFC), and walks the document
+  one token at a time, validating array indices against the RFC's `array-index` grammar and
+  rejecting missing object members or indices out of bounds.
+- Confirmed red before the implementation existed, then green after (`npm test`, 9/9 passing).
+- Verified `src/index.ts` type-checks cleanly against the project's existing strict
+  `tsconfig.json` (details and the import-extension tradeoff in decision-log
+  `dl-001-run-typescript-sources-directly-via-node-s-native-type-stripping-no-build-step`).
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `src/index.ts` (new): `resolvePointer` implementation.
+- `src/index.test.ts` (new): test suite.
